Here's a MATLAB program to convert an ultrasound signal from voltage vs time to magnitude (dB) vs frequency (MHz):

```matlab
function [freq_MHz, magnitude_dB] = ultrasound_fft_analysis(voltage, time)
% ULTRASOUND_FFT_ANALYSIS Convert ultrasound signal to frequency domain
%
% Inputs:
%   voltage - voltage signal array
%   time - time array (in seconds)
%
% Outputs:
%   freq_MHz - frequency array in MHz
%   magnitude_dB - magnitude in dB

% Calculate sampling parameters
N = length(voltage);                    % Number of samples
dt = time(2) - time(1);                % Sampling interval
fs = 1/dt;                             % Sampling frequency
df = fs/N;                             % Frequency resolution

% Perform FFT
Y = fft(voltage);                      % FFT of the signal
Y = Y(1:floor(N/2)+1);                 % Keep only positive frequencies

% Calculate frequency array in MHz
freq_Hz = (0:length(Y)-1) * df;        % Frequency in Hz
freq_MHz = freq_Hz / 1e6;              % Convert to MHz

% Calculate magnitude in dB
magnitude_linear = abs(Y);             % Linear magnitude
magnitude_linear(2:end-1) = 2*magnitude_linear(2:end-1); % Account for negative frequencies

% Convert to dB (with protection against log(0))
magnitude_dB = 20*log10(magnitude_linear + eps);

end

%% Main script example
% Clear workspace
clear; clc; close all;

% Example 1: Generate synthetic ultrasound signal
% Parameters for synthetic signal
fs = 100e6;                    % 100 MHz sampling frequency
t_duration = 10e-6;            % 10 microseconds duration
t = 0:1/fs:t_duration-1/fs;    % Time vector

% Create synthetic ultrasound signal (typical frequencies 2-10 MHz)
f1 = 3.5e6;                    % 3.5 MHz component
f2 = 7.5e6;                    % 7.5 MHz component
voltage = sin(2*pi*f1*t).*exp(-t*1e6) + 0.5*sin(2*pi*f2*t).*exp(-t*0.5e6);
voltage = voltage + 0.1*randn(size(t)); % Add noise

% Convert to frequency domain
[freq_MHz, magnitude_dB] = ultrasound_fft_analysis(voltage, t);

% Plot results
figure('Position', [100 100 1200 800]);

% Time domain plot
subplot(2,2,1);
plot(t*1e6, voltage);
grid on;
xlabel('Time (μs)');
ylabel('Voltage (V)');
title('Ultrasound Signal - Time Domain');

% Frequency domain plot (full range)
subplot(2,2,2);
plot(freq_MHz, magnitude_dB);
grid on;
xlabel('Frequency (MHz)');
ylabel('Magnitude (dB)');
title('Ultrasound Signal - Frequency Domain');

% Frequency domain plot (zoomed to ultrasound range)
subplot(2,2,3);
ultrasound_range = freq_MHz <= 20; % Focus on 0-20 MHz
plot(freq_MHz(ultrasound_range), magnitude_dB(ultrasound_range));
grid on;
xlabel('Frequency (MHz)');
ylabel('Magnitude (dB)');
title('Ultrasound Signal - Frequency Domain (0-20 MHz)');

% Spectrogram
subplot(2,2,4);
window_length = round(length(voltage)/10);
overlap = round(window_length/2);
[S, F, T] = spectrogram(voltage, window_length, overlap, [], fs);
imagesc(T*1e6, F/1e6, 20*log10(abs(S)));
axis xy;
colorbar;
xlabel('Time (μs)');
ylabel('Frequency (MHz)');
title('Spectrogram');

%% Example 2: Load and process real ultrasound data
% Uncomment and modify this section to load your actual data
%{
% Load your ultrasound data
% Assuming you have data in variables: time_data, voltage_data
load('your_ultrasound_data.mat'); % Replace with your data file

% Convert to frequency domain
[freq_MHz, magnitude_dB] = ultrasound_fft_analysis(voltage_data, time_data);

% Plot results
figure;
subplot(2,1,1);
plot(time_data*1e6, voltage_data);
xlabel('Time (μs)');
ylabel('Voltage (V)');
title('Measured Ultrasound Signal');
grid on;

subplot(2,1,2);
ultrasound_range = freq_MHz <= 15; % Adjust range as needed
plot(freq_MHz(ultrasound_range), magnitude_dB(ultrasound_range));
xlabel('Frequency (MHz)');
ylabel('Magnitude (dB)');
title('Frequency Spectrum');
grid on;
%}

%% Additional utility function for windowing
function [freq_MHz, magnitude_dB] = ultrasound_fft_windowed(voltage, time, window_type)
% Apply windowing before FFT to reduce spectral leakage
%
% window_type options: 'hann', 'hamming', 'blackman', 'rectangular'

if nargin < 3
    window_type = 'hann';
end

N = length(voltage);

% Apply window
switch lower(window_type)
    case 'hann'
        w = hann(N);
    case 'hamming'
        w = hamming(N);
    case 'blackman'
        w = blackman(N);
    case 'rectangular'
        w = ones(N,1);
    otherwise
        w = hann(N);
        warning('Unknown window type. Using Hann window.');
end

% Apply window to signal
voltage_windowed = voltage(:) .* w;

% Calculate sampling parameters
dt = time(2) - time(1);
fs = 1/dt;
df = fs/N;

% Perform FFT
Y = fft(voltage_windowed);
Y = Y(1:floor(N/2)+1);

% Compensate for window power loss
window_power = mean(w.^2);
Y = Y / sqrt(window_power);

% Calculate frequency array
freq_Hz = (0:length(Y)-1) * df;
freq_MHz = freq_Hz / 1e6;

% Calculate magnitude in dB
magnitude_linear = abs(Y);
magnitude_linear(2:end-1) = 2*magnitude_linear(2:end-1);
magnitude_dB = 20*log10(magnitude_linear + eps);

end
```

## Key Features:

1. **Main Function**: `ultrasound_fft_analysis()` performs the FFT conversion
2. **Proper FFT Processing**: Handles one-sided spectrum and frequency scaling
3. **dB Conversion**: Converts linear magnitude to decibels
4. **Example Script**: Demonstrates usage with synthetic ultrasound data
5. **Visualization**: Creates comprehensive plots including time domain, frequency domain, and spectrogram
6. **Windowing Function**: Optional windowed FFT to reduce spectral leakage

## Usage Instructions:

1. **For synthetic data**: Run the script as-is to see the example
2. **For real data**: Uncomment Example 2 section and load your data
3. **Required inputs**: 
   - `voltage`: array of voltage measurements
   - `time`: corresponding time array in seconds

## Important Notes:

- Ensure your time array is in seconds (the function converts to MHz automatically)
- The function adds `eps` to prevent `log(0)` errors
- Adjust the frequency range for plotting based on your ultrasound transducer specifications
- For better spectral analysis, consider using the windowed version for signals with finite duration

The program will output frequency in MHz and magnitude in dB, which is standard for ultrasound signal analysis.

